Q: Variable Copies in Multi-Threading foreach loops question A: <p>Strings are reference types, just like CustomClass,  and variable capture is an issue with all callbacks, no matter what the variable is. You need the inner copy.  You'd have the same problem with <code>int</code>. Besides, creating a local variable to a reference type costs nothing. You just store a 64-bit pointer to a RAM location.</p>
<p>That's the least of the problems though. This code is buggy and any measurements are meaningless.</p>
<p>There are no threads in the code. There's a <code>for</code> loop that fires of tasks at full CPU speed and never awaits them. Any measurements only measure how fast the loop can create new tasks, nothing more.</p>
<p>Tasks aren't threads, <code>Task.Run</code> doesn't start threads. Tasks <em>run on</em> threadpool threads, and threads run on cores. You can't run more threads than cores at any one time. If there are just a few tasks, they may take up all CPU cores. If there are more, some will wait until a core is released. If the application or request ends before some tasks are processed, they're lost.</p>
<p>A correct way to process the objects in parallel is using <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.threading.tasks.parallel.foreachasync?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Parallel.ForEachAsync</a>. Parallel.ForEachAsync will use all available cores by default but can be configured to use more or less. All callbacks get a CancellationToken, which means you can gracefully cancel too, whether the code is asynchronous or not :</p>
<pre><code>await Parallel.ForEachAsync(list, (item,cancellation)=&gt;{
    if (!cancellation.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        processTheItem(item);
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>The callback can be asynchronous too - remember, <code>await</code> awaits an already asynchronous operation, it doesn't start it. :</p>
<pre><code>await Parallel.ForEachAsync(list, async (item,cancellation)=&gt;{
    await processTheItemAsync(item,cancellation);
});
</code></pre>
<p><code>Parallel.ForEachAsync</code> can work on either an <code>IEnumerable</code> or an <code>IAsyncEnumerable</code>, so if <code>populateList();</code> is modified to return an <code>IAsyncEnumerable</code>, you can process items as they're produced, no need to wait for all to become available.</p>
<p>For example, using Dapper you can get results as an IAsyncEnumerable with <code>QueryUnbufferedAsync</code>.  :</p>
<pre><code>var resultStream=cnn.QueryUnbufferedAsync&lt;SomeClass&gt;(query);

await Parallel.ForEachAsync(resultStream, async (item,cancellation)=&gt;{
    await processTheItemAsync(item,cancellation);
});
</code></pre>

Q: Why my TableColumn in the Table doesn&#39;t resize dynamically when I change the length of the string and width property set to &quot;infinity&quot;? A: <p>In SwiftUI, <code>.width(min:ideal:max:)</code> <em><strong>doesn't automatically make the column scrollable or dynamically resize beyond the parent container.</strong></em></p>
<p><code>.infinity</code> in <code>max</code> means <strong>take all available space,</strong> <em><strong>not grow beyond parent if content is longer.</strong></em></p>
<p>Your <code>Table</code> is <strong>inside</strong> a <code>VStack</code> constrained by <code>.frame(width: 400)</code>, so SwiftUI assumes the table <em><strong>cannot exceed 400 points width</strong></em>. Hence, no horizontal scrolling occurs.</p>
<p>You need to <strong>wrap</strong> the <code>Table</code> in a <strong>scroll view</strong> with <code>.horizontal</code> enabled &amp; remove the fixed <code>.frame(width:)</code> (or let the scroll view handle overflow). Optionally you can set a minimum width for the column to let it shrink/expand naturally.</p>
<p>Your final code will be</p>
<pre><code>import SwiftUI
import Foundation

struct myItem: Identifiable {
    let id = UUID()
    let myItemStr: String
}

struct ContentView: View {
    @State private var myTableTitleStr: String = &quot;My Items&quot;
    @State private var selectedItemID: myItem.ID?
    @State private var myItemsArray = [
        myItem(myItemStr: &quot;Item01&quot;),
        myItem(myItemStr: &quot;Item02&quot;),
        myItem(myItemStr: &quot;Item03&quot;)
    ]

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            ScrollView([.horizontal, .vertical]) {  // &lt;- this will enable scrolling
                Table(myItemsArray, selection: $selectedItemID) {
                    TableColumn(myTableTitleStr, value: \.myItemStr)
                        .width(min: 50, ideal: 350) // remove .infinity
                }
            }
            .frame(height: 300) // limit height if needed

            Button(&quot;Add Long String&quot;) {
                let myLongString = &quot;my very very very very very very very very very very very very very very very very very long string&quot;
                myItemsArray[0] = myItem(myItemStr: myLongString)
            }
        }
        .padding()
    }
}
</code></pre>
